1
00:00:00,767 --> 00:00:08,522
This is Cedar: an open source assistant that runs on your own computer, and does real work for you.

2
00:00:09,723 --> 00:00:15,845
The first time you open it, Cedar asks which language you'd like to work in.

3
00:00:15,845 --> 00:00:20,743
After that it needs just one thing: a model to think with.

4
00:00:21,943 --> 00:00:24,800
Cedar isn't tied to one AI company.

5
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:28,882
Under Settings, Models, you choose where the model comes from.

6
00:00:28,882 --> 00:00:35,821
The Cedar app for Mac and Windows can download one and run it for you with llama.

7
00:00:35,821 --> 00:00:36,229
cpp.

8
00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:40,310
You can use a hosted provider with an API key.

9
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:45,208
Or you can point Cedar at a model server you already run.

10
00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:47,657
Here, that's vLLM on this machine.

11
00:00:47,657 --> 00:00:50,514
Cedar lists the models the server offers.

12
00:00:50,514 --> 00:00:57,861
Pick one, and it fills in the reasoning levels the model takes, and whether it can read images.

13
00:00:57,861 --> 00:01:01,943
Then it checks the address once more, and saves it.

14
00:01:04,659 --> 00:01:06,292
Now for a task.

15
00:01:06,292 --> 00:01:18,128
I'll attach a year of household spending, and ask Cedar to work out where the money goes, chart it, and write a one-page summary with three ways to save.

16
00:01:21,026 --> 00:01:22,659
Cedar doesn't just start.

17
00:01:22,659 --> 00:01:26,740
For a bigger task, it shows you its plan first.

18
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:31,230
You can reword a step, remove one, or add your own.

19
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:34,495
When it looks right, tell it to start.

20
00:01:36,893 --> 00:01:46,689
The work happens in a sealed box: a container of its own, with the network off, that can't see the rest of your computer.

21
00:01:46,689 --> 00:01:51,995
Cedar runs the numbers in Python, draws the chart, and writes the PDF.

22
00:01:51,995 --> 00:01:58,526
Each step stays in the conversation, so you can open it and see exactly what ran.

23
00:02:01,764 --> 00:02:06,662
The chart is interactive: point at a month to read every category.

24
00:02:06,662 --> 00:02:12,376
The finished summary sits on the right, with the three suggestions, ready to download.

25
00:02:12,376 --> 00:02:15,642
And the short version is in the conversation.

26
00:02:26,879 --> 00:02:30,144
Some things Cedar won't do on its own.

27
00:02:30,144 --> 00:02:35,042
Ask it to delete a file, and it stops and asks first.

28
00:02:35,042 --> 00:02:38,716
Downloads, installs and sending email work the same way.

29
00:02:38,716 --> 00:02:41,165
Nothing risky happens without your OK.

30
00:02:42,366 --> 00:02:45,631
Cedar also remembers what you ask it to.

31
00:02:45,631 --> 00:02:50,121
Under Settings, Memory, you see everything it has kept about you.

32
00:02:50,121 --> 00:02:53,795
Pin it, archive it, or remove it for good.

33
00:02:53,795 --> 00:02:56,652
It stays on your own Cedar server.

34
00:03:05,243 --> 00:03:07,284
Every ability has a switch.

35
00:03:07,284 --> 00:03:10,141
Reading web pages, weather, maps, directions, stocks.

36
00:03:10,141 --> 00:03:12,590
Turn on what Cedar may reach.

37
00:03:12,590 --> 00:03:14,223
Everything else stays off.

38
00:03:18,572 --> 00:03:23,470
And when a conversation did its job, turn it into an agent.

39
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:26,327
Cedar writes the prompt from the conversation.

40
00:03:26,327 --> 00:03:33,266
You choose what starts it: a schedule, a change it watches for, new mail, a file arriving.

41
00:03:33,266 --> 00:03:35,715
This one will run every month.

42
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:43,738
Cedar is free and open source.

43
00:03:43,738 --> 00:03:49,044
It runs on Mac, Windows and Linux, with apps for iPhone and Android.

44
00:03:49,044 --> 00:03:50,269
Your own assistant.

45
00:03:50,269 --> 00:03:52,718
Runs at home, answers to you.
